# Word lists and search order

## Model

The interpreter searches a **stack of word lists** (top first). New definitions go to the **compilation wordlist** (`get-current` / `set-current`), not necessarily the search-order top.

```forth
wordlist constant private-wid
private-wid set-current      \ compile into private list
private-wid >order           \ make it searchable
get-order set-order          \ save/restore search order portably
previous                     \ drop search-order entry
only forth                   \ reset to standard (Gforth)
```

## Hide implementation words

Pattern from manual §5.15.3 — helpers in a private vocabulary, API in the public list:

```forth
get-current ( wid )
vocabulary gc also gc definitions
\ ... helper words ...
( wid ) set-current
\ ... public API (calls helpers) ...
previous
```

## Tables and case-sensitive lists

`table` — case-sensitive wordlist for dispatch tables. `cs-wordlist` / `cs-vocabulary` — case-sensitive compilation/search contexts.

## Portability

Avoid legacy `find` in new code — semantics are underspecified. Prefer `find-name` and related words when meta-programming. For portable programs, minimize search-order tricks; document **Environmental dependencies** when using vocabularies.

See `forth-defining.mdc` for `' word Alias name` (same xt, new header; immediate flag is per-alias).
